From 552318c634c9991646ac673dde550882de5e92d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:16:49 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 098/119] fix: the sensor has a full well - highlights clip per
 channel after the white balance, so a blown neutral stays neutral

TEST-CHECKLIST F6: white balance 5600 -> 3200 K turns the CLIPPED highlights blue.
The Bradford matrix is right; what is missing is a sensor. With nothing clipping
between the lights and the ODT, a sky 10^5 over mid grey keeps its channel RATIO
through a per-channel white-balance gain, and the ODT carries that ratio to the
display. Every RAW converter clips at a white level AFTER the WB multipliers for
exactly this reason.

FilmMapping's scene-referred branch gains step 1b, immediately after the Bradford
matrix and before the ACEScct encode: c = min(c, 0.18 * 2^N) per channel, one
common level for the three. N is a new ccam property, "Sensor Clip (stops over
grey)" ('sclp', Exposure group, 8..20, default 14) - the same 14 the exposure
meter's ceiling uses, so meter saturation and picture saturation agree. It travels
on the bus as Global_User_SensorClipStops (no transition, default 0 = off) and
reaches the shader in the previously unused SceneWBRow0.w, because the clip
belongs to the step those rows carry. Not gated by Bypass Grade: the sensor is not
a creative decision.

The lens effects upstream stay UNCLIPPED on purpose - bloom, glare, diffraction
and DOF see the true radiance, because a sun 10^5 over the clip must throw a 10^5
flare. The EXR tap is downstream of the clip and inherits it, which is what makes
a capture match the picture.

decisions/s8-sensor-clip.md records the two approaches not taken: clipping in
"sensor native" units before the WB (no such space exists here, and it would need
highlight reconstruction to undo the tint it creates), and a soft shoulder (does
not fix the tint, and puts a second tone curve in front of the ODT's).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
